# One table, many scales — 2026-11-11
#
# A reading of this repo's highlighters, caches, nesting theory and graded indexes beside two mounted consumers' latest work, with proposals for operator consistency, highlighting, hyperbolic nesting, graded indexing, cache tiers, and updates that teach whoever reads them next.
# The owner asked for hyperbolic nesting (first written as fractal design, corrected by the owner the same day, before this entry was committed), operator consistency, gradient indexing, syntax highlighting advancements and relationships to cache tiers; reviews of the recent changes in the site consumer and a second mounted consumer; and enhancements that make updates to Spw codebases more metacognitively valuable. The site consumer's session wrote a cache for this repo the same afternoon (workbench-interaction-theory-2026-11 in its .spw/caches), and this entry answers it.

#>journal_2026_11_11_one_table_many_scales
#:cache #!observation
#:layer #!pragmatics

^["entry"]{
 date: "2026-11-11"
 kind: #observation
 base: "main@5b421b8f with the address-grain-edit entry uncommitted; the site consumer at ef961d3b plus its uncommitted interaction-theory cache; a second mounted consumer at db9b613 plus a working tree that grew from 37 to 51 entries while it was read"
 method: "five readers: this repo's highlighters, caches, scale and graded indexes; the site consumer's 53 commits of 2026-11-10; its 19 caches of that day; the second consumer's 14 commits and working tree; and the interaction-theory cache read in full here. The two most severe review findings in each consumer were re-read at their lines"
 limits: "the reviews skipped some large commits and most prose; nothing was run in the second consumer; its working tree belongs to another session and changed during the read"
}

^["reviews"]{
 # Findings that bear on the workbench; the full lists went to the owner.
 site_consumer: .{
  root_negotiation: "spw.quest answers / with the text index when Accept names text/plain anywhere, so a client sending application/json, text/plain, */* gets text where it got quest.json (workers/spw-quest/src/index.js 681, wantsText before wantsJson; re-read)"
  rest_links: "four pages link to /physics?line= without asking physicsHref, so the llms.txt example rpgwednesday<card> links to a refusal (answer.js 215, build.js 555 and 567, pivot.js 461)"
  offline: "the offline page promises pages and a pinned parser the shell does not keep, and SHELL_VERSION did not move, so installed apps never get the new page"
  at_law: "physics returns a reference as a leaf before checking for a body, so @pantry{rice, oats} is refused although the law table says @ gathers"
  night_keys: "the night keys an unnamed probe by line number, so adding a line above it reports one probe new and another moved (scripts/lib/sleep-cycle.mjs 47 to 55)"
  crash_line: "the paste line takes the first tagged line, which is now a check that passed"
  episode_scope: "the genre check looks for scene, change and verify anywhere in the file, not in each #[episode] block"
  learning: "all 53 commits carry one episode; the best verify lines state their limits; six commits under the docs sigil changed code; corrections are overwritten in place; an ~#answer names a date and not the commit of the code it read; four reader builds are in service and nothing records which one stands behind a claim"
 }
 story_consumer: .{
  check_staleness: "npm run check compares git status rows before and after, so a generated file that was already modified looks unchanged however its contents move (book/scripts/tools/check.mjs 36 to 41; re-read)"
  residency_race: "uncommitted: two folios can be granted in one carry, since the found check runs before an await and the mark after it"
  plan_gate_ci: "uncommitted: on a shallow clone the gate calls every file not yet committed and skips the receipt check without saying so; it passes when it matches nothing"
  promise_gaps: "uncommitted: reading never finds a folio though the switch says it does; the desk refuses @[residency], which the module accepts"
  readers: "the probe that counts hand-written readers greps matchAll and misses parseWay and the residency regexes; the gate parses each file and then reads five fields by regex; a ^board line is the one Spw line in the game not drawn through renderSpell"
  learning: "commit messages are one subject line of up to 1,367 characters with no body; two of four ~#expect rows say not written down at the time; grants exist for 3 of 14 commits and link to no plan slice; eight dated probe runs are staged for deletion while the line that explains it is not"
 }
}

^["finding"]{
 operator_words: "the words for each mark are kept in at least four tables here (SIGIL_SEMANTICS in the LSP and again in the VS Code extension, OPERATOR_PAYLOADS in the CLI, OPERATOR_READER_NAMES in seed), three in spw.quest, one in the canvas, and a peer kit the site published as spwashi.peer-kit/1 calls ^ = % integration, binding and normalize where the workbench says ascension, configuration and measure; the site's kernel says so in a comment; a cache of 2026-11-10 contradicts the table it cites for . = $, and ~ carries five or more roles across that day's caches"
 painters: "every highlighter is written by hand: a TextMate grammar shared with IntelliJ, a per-line regex scanner for semantic tokens that does not use the lexer, a decoration layer, a Vim syntax file, three painters in the site consumer and a hand tokenizer in the second; they disagree on ! and ? (one LSP type), ~ @ $ (one LSP type), valence words (painted as !), < > against <> (which the lexer separates), stream prose and inline comments; nothing paints [reg=…], depth or whether a citation resolves"
 tiers: "about a dozen caches keep separate clocks (beats, request epochs, wall seconds, file mtimes, dates) and share only the 8/64/256 tiers from the ocean's cache algorithm; the theory gives four planes and lists five, seed's cache-layer names a different four, threshold-distance says five ladders and lists nine; fixity names both an operator's placement and a surface's stability; the collate bank has no cap the theory says it has"
 nesting: "Spw's nesting and its references branch: in the second consumer's book, things one, two and three steps away average 6.5, 38.5 and 107.6 (its spw-architecture.spw ^geometry), so a flat layout crowds by the second ring. Text gives each level of nesting linear room, which fits the site's findings that its reader stops at the first nested pair, no file it measured went past two levels, and authors write flat labels and nest only in play. The second consumer already draws its book on a Poincaré disk (spw-projection-disk.mjs, 1,441 lines: rings at tanh(d·step/2), re-centring by the Möbius map, walks along geodesics, a node's size the conformal factor 1 − |z|²) and folds its semantic index the same way (lore-fold.mjs); depiction-frames' fourth wonder expects exactly this host. Here, swap-grace keeps a nested cursor inspectable at any depth, threshold-distance draws one hop at the rim and three as fog, and no code computes a hyperbolic distance or lays out by one"
 gradient_algebra: "the second consumer's fold reads a Spw expression as a query whose answer is a gradient over its index: writing terms side by side sums them, &[a, b] keeps the weakest strength where all run together, ^ lifts to the strongest, % shows the numbers, ! offers the strongest place first; it is the one place found where operators already combine graded values"
 graded: "evictionScore, the liminality order, the beat tiers, geometric resonance and a did-you-mean scorer are graded; the five distances, a reach product, approximate selection and lattice scores are theory only; a bounded BFS exists and nothing reports hops; a selector match is yes or no"
 learning_here: "199 of the last 200 commits carry an episode block, 180 parse clean and 155 carry scene, change and verify; the commit hook finds the block by regex and never parses it (decision-trail ^episode_corpus)"
}

^["proposals"]{
 operator_table: .{
  says: "one table in seed, exported: for each mark its glyph, its workbench word, its reader name, its law as a body feels it (the canvas's LAW_SAYS), its highlight role, and the names of each container slot in both columns (mark, word, concept, scene, mode, definition beside charge, subject, projection, scope, frame, body); a host that keeps a different word declares it as an alias with the host's name, so a difference is a row and not a drift"
  generates: "the TextMate grammar, the Vim syntax groups, the semantic-token legend and modifiers, hover text, the quest guide's words and the CLI payloads; a test in each host diffs its copy against the table until the copy is deleted"
  answers: "finding operator_words; the interaction cache's first start_small row (one operator-word table, three hosts gain); audit-ui-data-models ~[table_words_export] and ~[client_palettes_from_table]"
  open: "audit-ui-data-models ?[words_travel]: which words travel with a sigil into every client, and which stay a host's presentation; this entry adds the peer kit's published words as evidence"
  home: ~"../../../.agents/plans/audit-ui-data-models/wip.spw"
 }
 paint_by_meaning: .{
  says: "one tokenizer, the lexer, behind every painter, with the regex scanner retired; roles from the operator table; then modifiers that carry meaning and not glyph: container kind, distance from the reader's focus as a gradient of lightness (hyperbolic_nesting), a citation resolved, unresolved or unverified, an address's stability (label, key, hash, ordinal), a register stamp that restates its bracket faded and one that informs kept; the site's typography rules become a law every painter keeps: a dimmed part keeps its width, a digraph is never kerned apart, and colour names what a seat sees without moving a column"
  web: "highlightHtml from the bundle is the one web painter; the site's three painters and the second consumer's hand tokenizer read it"
  answers: "finding painters; the interaction cache's editor rows (hover a body's word and read its law, from one table both read)"
  home: ~"../../../.agents/plans/editor-reading-hierarchy/wip.spw"
 }
 hyperbolic_nesting: .{
  says: "read nesting from a focus, the way the second consumer's disk reads its book: the construct a reader holds is the centre, and every other construct sits at its tree distance from it (depth to the shared ancestor and back down, computed from two addresses by their shared label path). Detail falls with that distance as size falls on the disk: the held construct whole, its siblings as their one-line summary, theirs as a count, the rest folded toward a rim. Moving the focus is a re-centring, the Möbius map in a drawing and a refold in text, and nothing is dropped: a construct at the rim keeps its address and can be made the centre in one act (swap-grace's recursive presence)"
  kernel: "lift the disk's geometry (mobius, alongGeodesic, fromCentre, geodesic, a ring layout) from the second consumer into a host-neutral module beside the ocean's distance work, with its tests; that consumer keeps building its book's graph, and the canvas and spw.quest get rim, fog and a pivot ring from the same function"
  two_trees: "containment (what nests in what) and citation (what points at what) are two graphs; both can be laid on one disk, as joints and as sight lines (depiction-frames), and neither distance stands in for the other (operational-topography)"
  editors: "text is flat, so in an editor hyperbolic nesting is focus folding: a custom LSP request returns, for a position and a radius, each construct's range and its detail (whole, summary, count, folded); VS Code applies it with folding and a fade, Neovim with foldexpr and a highlight namespace, and both refold as the caret moves; lightness by distance from the focus replaces absolute depth in paint_by_meaning"
  cli: "spw skim --focus <address> --radius r prints the same reading: the held construct whole, near ones as lines, far ones as counts"
  interest: "Furnas's fisheye views (1986) score a thing's degree of interest as its importance less its distance from the focus; the index keeps importance per address (inbound citations, fixity, recency) and one_distance supplies the distance, so ranking, folding and the disk share one score"
  grounded: "Lamping and Rao's hyperbolic browser (1995) gave a large hierarchy focus and context at once; Poincaré embeddings (Nickel and Kiela, 2017) keep a hierarchy's distances in few dimensions, where a flat plane cannot"
  probe: "depiction-frames' fourth wonder, run in an editor as well as on a disk: a held line and thirty surfaces one to three hops away; time finding a named one with flat folds and with focus folds"
  answers: "finding nesting; the site's flat labels and two-level files; spw.quest's hold, which survives only at depth 0; the interaction cache's pivot ring and rim and fog"
  open: ~"../../../.agents/plans/interpretation-trials/interpretation-trials.spw#wonder_trial_grain"
  home: ~"../../../.agents/plans/line-kit/wip.spw"
 }
 gradient_algebra: .{
  says: "record the second consumer's fold algebra as a reading of what operators do to graded values: side by side sums, & takes the weakest, ^ the strongest, % shows the measure, ! offers the strongest first; if it holds, a selector's approximate tier (one_distance) is written in Spw itself, and the operator table gains a column for what each mark does to a gradient"
  watch: "^ as the strongest is the peer kit's integration, not seed's ascension; the reading is evidence for ?[words_travel], not a decision"
 }
 one_distance: .{
  says: "one distance function over addresses, as the interaction cache and threshold-distance propose: hops along citations from the canon root and from a reader's centre, shared label-path prefix, depth, vocabulary by slotHash vectors (geometric resonance already computes the cosine), and time by as_of or commit age; weighted three ways the cache names, memory, index and stage; every index row carries its reach, and a selector gains an approximate tier that ranks instead of matching"
  uses: "an inlay or lens with hops to the root, completion and did-you-mean ranked by it, eviction scored by it (far, cold and uncoupled goes first), the canvas's rim and fog, and a mount that reports what of the workbench it reaches"
  home_of_code: ".spw/biome/ocean, beside the cache algorithm it would replace a constant in, as the interaction cache asks"
  answers: "finding graded; the site's 75 pairs one slot apart of which 66 have no door"
 }
 cache_registry: .{
  says: "every cache declares itself in one line: what it keys on, which clock it runs on, its tiers, what invalidates it, where it lives, and until when it keeps; spw inspect lists them from the registry; the theory's counts are re-read against it and corrected in a dated entry; one of the two meanings of fixity takes another word"
  kept_line: "the same line, shown to a reader, is the interaction cache's kept line per host: what is kept, where, until when, and how to forget it, with a receipt for what was let go"
  three_kinds: "computed caches (keyed, evictable, under .spw/gen), kept records (dated, append-only, under .spw/caches) and canon (on a fixity ladder) are three kinds with three freshness rules; a host's cache index that misses rows (the site's registry lacks 11 of 19) is caught by a check, not by a reader"
  answers: "finding tiers; the interaction cache's memory facet"
 }
 reader_stamp: .{
  says: "every reader bundle carries the workbench commit and a content hash; a host's check compares its bundles with its mount pin and lists any build behind it; a claim, an ~#answer and a verify line name the reader build they read with"
  answers: "the site's four reader builds in service with no record of which stands behind a claim, and its mount that moved without a rebuild; the second consumer's bundle verify, which already does half of this"
 }
 updates_that_teach: .{
  says: "the commit hook parses the episode it finds and warns on what will not parse; an episode may add a guess made before (~#expect), what differed (?[surprise]), what was checked to stay true as probes (.[held]), and for a refactor what it kept, dropped and how to come back; a correction is a new row that names what it corrects, never an overwrite; a docs sigil on a commit that changes code earns a warning"
  questions_wake: "a wonder may name the files it is about (~#about), and the commit hook lists the open questions about the files a commit touches, so the moment of change is the moment a question is asked again; a night run keys probes by content, runs each twice before trusting a change, and keeps its receipts where git can see them"
  history: "spw history <address> lists the episodes that touched an address with their guesses and surprises, so a reader can see when a belief about it changed"
  answers: "finding learning_here; both reviews' learning rows; the site's dreamable bar (a guess on record, a check that runs, stable output, a way to let go); the interaction cache's refactoring facet"
  home: ~"../../../.agents/plans/inquiry-loop/wip.spw"
 }
}

^["answers_to_the_interaction_cache"]{
 # Row by row, what this entry and the one before it say to the site's cache for the workbench.
 operator_word_table: "proposals operator_table and paint_by_meaning"
 kept_line: "proposal cache_registry, kept_line"
 distance_in_the_ocean: "proposal one_distance"
 pivot_ring: "a pivot is one slot changed, which the line kit's readAt and writeAt make; the address-grain-edit entry's grain and edit_verbs give it a step and an undo; hyperbolic_nesting gives it a rim to sit on"
 refactor_receipts: "address-grain-edit edit_verbs (each verb returns its inverse and its delta) and updates_that_teach"
 editors_show_the_canvas: "paint_by_meaning for laws, one_distance for reach; opening the canvas on a line stays the canvas's door"
 mount_reach: "one_distance uses, and reader_stamp"
 minds: "each tool owning one question is the host's to keep; the workbench's part is that every receipt is a Spw line any other tool can read"
}

^["order"]{
 steps: ["operator_table, since highlighters, hovers and three hosts' words wait on it", "updates_that_teach, starting with parsing the episode in the hook", "cache_registry and reader_stamp", "paint_by_meaning once the table exists", "one_distance", "hyperbolic_nesting: the kernel lift early, since a host already proves it; focus folding with the grain ladder", "gradient_algebra once ?[words_travel] is answered"]
 owner: ["which word wins where a host's word differs, or whether both stand as aliases (audit-ui-data-models ?[words_travel])", "which meaning of fixity keeps the word", "whether an episode's new parts are optional forever or become expected for code commits"]
}
